import json, pathlib, re
CH = [
 ("The parking lot", """[serious] On the afternoon of July thirtieth, 1975, a sixty-two-year-old man in a dark blue shirt paced a restaurant parking lot in the suburbs of Detroit. He was waiting for a meeting. At around a quarter past two, he walked to a pay phone and called his wife. Nobody had shown up, he told her. He was being stood up. A little later, he was gone. His car was found the next morning, unlocked, in the same parking lot. No note. No struggle. No body. The man was Jimmy Hoffa, once the most powerful labor leader in America. More than fifty years, hundreds of FBI agents, and dozens of digs later, he has never been found. This is the story of how Hoffa rose, how he fell, and what the evidence actually says about one of the most famous disappearances in American history."""),
 ("The rise", """James Riddle Hoffa was born in Brazil, Indiana, in 1913. His father died when he was a child, and the family moved to Detroit. He left school at fourteen and went to work. As a teenager, loading trucks at a grocery warehouse, he helped organize his first strike. By 1932, he was organizing for the International Brotherhood of Teamsters, the union for truck drivers and warehouse workers. It was rough work in a rough era. Strikes could turn violent, and Hoffa was arrested many times. But he was relentless, and he was good at it. He helped build the Teamsters into a national force, and in 1957, he became the union's president. Under Hoffa, the Teamsters grew to roughly two million members. He negotiated the first national contract for truck drivers. To many members, he was a hero who got them better pay. To his critics, he was something else. Because to build that power, Hoffa had built ties with organized crime."""),
 ("The feud", """By the late 1950s, a Senate committee was investigating corruption in labor unions. Its chief counsel was a young lawyer named Robert Kennedy. Kennedy and Hoffa despised each other from the start. Hoffa later called it a blood feud. The hearings accused Hoffa of ties to organized crime and of misusing union money, and the AFL-CIO expelled the Teamsters. When Kennedy became Attorney General in 1961, the Justice Department went after Hoffa with a dedicated team of prosecutors. In 1964, Hoffa was convicted twice. First of jury tampering, for trying to bribe a juror in an earlier trial. Then of fraud, over the misuse of the union's pension fund. That pension fund matters. It held hundreds of millions of dollars, and it had been lending money to projects linked to the mob, including casinos in Las Vegas. Whoever controlled the Teamsters controlled access to that money. In March 1967, after his appeals ran out, Jimmy Hoffa went to federal prison."""),
 ("The deal", """Even behind bars, Hoffa stayed president of the Teamsters. A loyal ally, Frank Fitzsimmons, ran the union day to day. Then, in 1971, Hoffa resigned the presidency, and Fitzsimmons took over. Months later, in December 1971, President Richard Nixon commuted Hoffa's sentence. He walked free. But there was a catch. The commutation barred him from taking part in union management until 1980. Hoffa said he never agreed to that condition, and he fought it in court. He lost. And he made no secret of his plans. He wanted his union back. The problem was, the people who had been doing very well under Fitzsimmons, including powerful figures in organized crime, did not want him back. With Fitzsimmons, they had easy access to the pension fund. Hoffa was talking about exposing mob influence in the union, and the more he talked, the more dangerous he became to some very dangerous people."""),
 ("The last afternoon", """Which brings us back to July thirtieth, 1975. Hoffa had written a note in his calendar for a two o'clock meeting at the Machus Red Fox restaurant in Bloomfield Township, Michigan. The men he expected were Anthony Giacalone, a reputed Detroit mob figure, and Anthony Provenzano, a New Jersey Teamsters leader with alleged mob ties. The meeting was supposed to settle their differences. Neither of them came. Both later denied that any meeting had been set. Provenzano had an alibi in New Jersey. Giacalone was seen at a health club. After Hoffa's call to his wife, he was last seen around a quarter to three. There was no sign of a struggle, as if he had climbed into a car with someone he knew. One reported witness said he saw Hoffa in the back of a maroon car. Investigators zeroed in on a maroon 1975 Mercury, borrowed that day by Charles O'Brien, a man Hoffa had treated almost like a son. Police dogs picked up Hoffa's scent in the car. In 2001, DNA testing matched a hair from that car to Hoffa, though it could not show when it got there. O'Brien always denied any involvement, and he was never charged."""),
 ("The search", """The investigation was enormous. Hundreds of FBI agents worked the case, producing thousands of pages of files. A 1976 FBI summary, known as the Hoffex memo, concluded that Hoffa was probably killed by organized crime, because of his efforts to regain control of the union. Several suspects went to prison on other charges. Nobody was ever charged with his murder. In 1982, Hoffa was declared legally dead. Then came the digs. A claim pointed to Giants Stadium in New Jersey. When it was torn down in 2010, nothing turned up. In 2004, police took up floorboards from a Detroit house, after a self-described mob hit man named Frank Sheeran claimed he shot Hoffa there. The blood was not Hoffa's. Sheeran's story later became the Martin Scorsese film The Irishman. Many historians doubt it. In 2006, the FBI dug up a horse farm outside Detroit for two weeks. Nothing. In 2013, they dug up a field north of the city. Nothing. In 2021, the FBI surveyed a former landfill under a highway bridge in Jersey City, after a man said his father confessed on his deathbed that Hoffa was buried there in a steel drum. In 2022, the FBI announced it had found nothing of evidentiary value."""),
 ("Our analysis", """Here's how we read the evidence. The most likely explanation is still the one in the FBI's own memo. Jimmy Hoffa was killed because he was trying to take back a union that powerful people wanted to keep. The details point to a setup. A meeting with men who never showed up. No struggle. A car he may have ridden in, though the man who borrowed it always denied any role. Investigators believed he got in willingly. What happened next is where the evidence runs out. Every confession has been doubted, and every dig has come up empty. Most of the men suspected are now dead. The case is still officially open. But fifty years on, the hard truth may be that the people who knew where Jimmy Hoffa is took the answer with them. Where do you think he is? Tell me in the comments. This is Dossier Decoded."""),
]
